{"name":"Big Bus Yellow","hex":"#ffda8b","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"big","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Biangai."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of great size, large.","Fat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/big"},{"term":"bus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Bokobaru."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A motor vehicle for transporting large numbers of people along roads.","A coach, a bus used for long travels."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bus"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Big Bus Tours","blocks":[{"text":"Big Bus Tours, is an operator of open top bus sightseeing tours formed in May 2011 after \"Les Cars Rouges\" and the \"Big Bus Company\" merged. The company operates in 28 cities of 18 countries with 444 buses operating around the world. Most of the cities it serves are in the United States. Typically, Big Bus Tours use open top double-decker buses in burgundy and cream-coloured livery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/ef/BigBus.svg/330px-BigBus.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Bus_Tours","wikidata":"Q6980608"},{"title":"Bus","blocks":[{"text":"A bus is a motor vehicle that carries significantly more passengers than an average car or van, but fewer than the average rail transport. It is most commonly used in public transport, but is also in use for charter purposes, or through private ownership. Although buses typically carry between 30 and 100 passengers, some buses have a capacity of up to 300 passengers. The most common type is the single-deck rigid bus, with double-decker and articulated buses carrying larger loads, and midibuses and minibuses carrying smaller loads. Coaches are used for longer-distance services. Many types of buses, such as city transit buses and inter-city coaches, charge a fare. Other types, such as elementary or secondary school buses or shuttle buses within a post-secondary education campus, are free. In many jurisdictions, bus drivers require a special large vehicle licence above and beyond a regular driving license."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/LTZ1328-19-20241030-160332.jpg/330px-LTZ1328-19-20241030-160332.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bus","wikidata":"Q5638"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow","wikidata":"Q943"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/CDG_Big_Bus_E4_GP-123-JM_2023-11-12.jpg/500px-CDG_Big_Bus_E4_GP-123-JM_2023-11-12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/CDG_Big_Bus_E4_GP-123-JM_2023-11-12.jpg/1280px-CDG_Big_Bus_E4_GP-123-JM_2023-11-12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/CDG_Big_Bus_E4_GP-123-JM_2023-11-12.jpg/500px-CDG_Big_Bus_E4_GP-123-JM_2023-11-12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACDG_Big_Bus_E4_GP-123-JM_2023-11-12.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Big_bus_co_at_speakers_corner.jpg/500px-Big_bus_co_at_speakers_corner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Big_bus_co_at_speakers_corner.jpg/500px-Big_bus_co_at_speakers_corner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Big Bus company kiosk and bus queue at Speakers' Corner, London","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_bus_co_at_speakers_corner.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SH6230_at_Central_Ferry_Pier_%2820181009145722%29.jpg/500px-SH6230_at_Central_Ferry_Pier_%2820181009145722%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/SH6230_at_Central_Ferry_Pier_%2820181009145722%29.jpg/1280px-SH6230_at_Central_Ferry_Pier_%2820181009145722%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte